Content:
 1  # Lohoff 2e spatial-heterogeneity statistic computation
 2  
 3  ## Inputs
 4  - Dataset: `library/datasets/dataset_lohoff_et_al_seqfish.h5ad`
 5  - Matrix: `adata.X`, shape 19,416 cells x 351 genes, converted from sparse to dense for vectorized linear models.
 6  - Cell-type annotation: `obs['celltype_mapped_refined']` (22 observed categories).
 7  - Spatial coordinates: `obsm['spatial']`.
 8  - Embryo and z-slice covariates: parsed from observation names matching `^(?P<embryo>embryo\d+)_Pos(?P<position>\d+)_cell(?P<cell>\d+)_z(?P<z>\d+)$`.
 9  
10  ## Statistical method implemented
11  For each cell type and gene, cells of that type were used as observations.  A spatial covariate was first computed as the row-normalized inverse-shortest-path weighted average of the same gene's expression in all other cells from the same cell type.  The cell-cell neighborhood network was reconstructed with a Delaunay triangulation on the supplied two-dimensional spatial coordinates because the h5ad object did not contain a precomputed neighborhood graph (`obsp` was empty).  The distance D_jk was the topological shortest-path length on this graph; self weights were set to zero, and each row of weights was normalized to sum to one to match the stated weighted-mean covariate.
12  
13  The requested linear model was then fit gene-by-gene:
14  
15  `expression ~ spatial_weighted_expression + embryo + z_slice + embryo:z_slice`.
16  
17  Only one embryo (`embryo1`) is present in this staged dataset, so embryo main effects and embryo:z interactions are not separately estimable.  The implementation builds the available non-spatial covariate design and uses SVD rank reduction; in practice the estimable non-spatial adjustment is the z-slice indicator plus intercept for cell types containing both z slices.  The reported statistic is the t-statistic for the spatial weighted-expression coefficient.
18  
19  ## Ordering variable
20  Cell types were ordered by the mean t-statistic over all 351 genes, ignoring non-estimable values.
21  
