# Lohoff 2e spatial-heterogeneity statistic computation

## Inputs
- Dataset: `library/datasets/dataset_lohoff_et_al_seqfish.h5ad`
- Matrix: `adata.X`, shape 19,416 cells x 351 genes, converted from sparse to dense for vectorized linear models.
- Cell-type annotation: `obs['celltype_mapped_refined']` (22 observed categories).
- Spatial coordinates: `obsm['spatial']`.
- Embryo and z-slice covariates: parsed from observation names matching `^(?P<embryo>embryo\d+)_Pos(?P<position>\d+)_cell(?P<cell>\d+)_z(?P<z>\d+)$`.

## Statistical method implemented
For each cell type and gene, cells of that type were used as observations.  A spatial covariate was first computed as the row-normalized inverse-shortest-path weighted average of the same gene's expression in all other cells from the same cell type.  The cell-cell neighborhood network was reconstructed with a Delaunay triangulation on the supplied two-dimensional spatial coordinates because the h5ad object did not contain a precomputed neighborhood graph (`obsp` was empty).  The distance D_jk was the topological shortest-path length on this graph; self weights were set to zero, and each row of weights was normalized to sum to one to match the stated weighted-mean covariate.

The requested linear model was then fit gene-by-gene:

`expression ~ spatial_weighted_expression + embryo + z_slice + embryo:z_slice`.

Only one embryo (`embryo1`) is present in this staged dataset, so embryo main effects and embryo:z interactions are not separ

